---
// Catch broken Markdown links in GitHub Actions: checked against the validate
// and audit docs and their source on main (cli/commands/validate.ts,
// core/links.ts, core/probe.ts, extractLinks in core/sources/normalize.ts,
// cli/commands/audit.ts, audit/checks/links.ts, audit/checks/network.ts,
// audit/catalog.ts, audit/terms.ts), the custom anchors and redirects docs,
// GitHub's docs on workflow commands, the schedule event, and required status
// checks, and GitHub's Actions workshop. Ran in a scratch fixture with the
// local CLI build: `blume validate`, `--strict`, and `--json` against the
// good, breaking, and fixed commits (the output below is copied from it), the
// annotation and summary scripts under `bash -e`, `blume validate --external`
// against a 404, a 429, an unresolvable host, and a live URL, and
// `blume audit --only` with the ids below (accepted, then "No build found").
// validate has since learned reference-style links, autolinks, raw `<a>`
// tags, and link labels that wrap onto a second line. validate and audit
// have since taken a repeatable `--ignore <glob>` (cli/args.ts), run with the
// local CLI build against a local server: matched URLs were never requested
// and weren't reported. The workflows,
// `blume build`, and a full `blume audit` were not run. Review: rechecked the
// grading (core/probe.ts), the link scanner (MD_LINK and COMPONENT_HREF in
// core/sources/normalize.ts), and the audit ids and --fail-on gate; built
// metadata checks stay in documentation-seo-audit-ci. Not yet run end to end.
import CodeBlock from "blume/components/content/CodeBlock.astro";
import GuidePage from "../_guides/GuidePage.astro";
import { guideById } from "../_guides/guides.ts";
---

<GuidePage guide={guideById("markdown-link-checking-ci")}>
  <p>
    To make a pull request fail when it breaks a docs link, run{" "}
    <code>blume validate --strict</code> in a GitHub Actions workflow on every
    pull request, and make that check required. It reads your Markdown and
    MDX, resolves each internal link and <code>#fragment</code> against the
    pages and headings your site actually has, and exits non-zero on a miss.
    Don't leave out <code>--strict</code>: a broken anchor is a warning, and
    warnings alone don't fail the command.
  </p>
  <p>
    By the end, you have a small repository whose pull request fails on a
    renamed page and a reworded heading, with each broken link annotated on its
    line, and passes once they're fixed. A second check reads the built HTML,
    and a weekly job probes external URLs without blocking anyone's merge.
  </p>
  <p>
    This covers docs built with Blume. If the Markdown you want checked is
    READMEs spread across a repository rather than a docs site, a general link
    checker such as{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/lycheeverse/lychee">Lychee</a> fits better,
    because <code>blume validate</code> only reads your site's content.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up the example</h2>
  <p>
    The example is a three-page docs site for Acme's CLI, laid out the way{" "}
    <code>npx blume init</code> scaffolds it, at the root of the repository:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docs/
  configuration.mdx
  index.mdx
  installation.mdx
blume.config.ts
package.json
package-lock.json`}
    lang="txt"
  />
  <p>Two pages link to a section of the configuration page:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`---
title: Acme docs
description: Send transactional email and SMS with the Acme Messages API.
---

Start by [installing the CLI](/installation), then [create an API key](/configuration#api-keys).`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/index.mdx"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    code={`---
title: Installation
description: Install the Acme CLI on macOS, Linux, or Windows.
---

## Install the CLI

Install the CLI with your package manager, then [set your API key](./configuration#api-keys).`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/installation.mdx"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    code={`---
title: Configuration
description: Configure the Acme CLI with an API key and a default region.
---

## API keys

Create a key in the dashboard and export it as \`ACME_API_KEY\`.

## Regions

Pick the region closest to your users.`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/configuration.mdx"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    code={`import { defineConfig } from "blume";

export default defineConfig({
  title: "Acme",
  description: "Send transactional email and SMS with the Acme Messages API.",
});`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    Commit this to <code>main</code>, then run the check once. It prints{" "}
    <code>No broken links found.</code> and exits 0.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code="npx blume validate --strict" lang="bash" />

  <h2>Break a link and an anchor</h2>
  <p>
    Now make the kind of change that breaks links in real repositories. On a
    branch, rename the installation page:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`git switch -c rename-install
git mv docs/installation.mdx docs/install.mdx`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    Then reword the heading the other pages link to: change{" "}
    <code>## API keys</code> to <code>## Authentication</code> in{" "}
    <code>docs/configuration.mdx</code>. Commit both changes and run the check
    without <code>--strict</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`git commit -am "Rename the install page and the API keys section"
npx blume validate`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    code={`BLUME_BROKEN_LINK Broken link to /installation: no page resolves to /installation.
  at docs/index.mdx:6:31
  fix: Check the path, or create the target page.
  docs: https://useblume.dev/docs/cli/validate
BLUME_BROKEN_ANCHOR No anchor target on /configuration matches #api-keys.
  at docs/index.mdx:6:72
  docs: https://useblume.dev/docs/cli/validate
BLUME_BROKEN_ANCHOR No anchor target on /configuration matches #api-keys.
  at docs/install.mdx:8:68
  docs: https://useblume.dev/docs/cli/validate

1 error(s), 2 warning(s)`}
    lang="txt"
  />
  <p>
    The rename left a link to a route no page serves, which is an error. The
    new heading generates the id <code>authentication</code>, so both links to{" "}
    <code>#api-keys</code> now land at the top of the page instead of the
    section. That's a warning, because the page itself still loads. Each
    finding names the file, line, and column to fix.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's why <code>--strict</code> matters. Fix only the page link and the
    command exits 0 with two warnings. With <code>--strict</code>, warnings
    fail it too. Info-level notes never do, like{" "}
    <code>BLUME_ASSETS_UNCHECKED</code>, which says asset links went unchecked
    because there's no <code>public/</code> folder.
  </p>
  <p>
    A link counts as valid when it lands on a Markdown page, a custom{" "}
    <code>.astro</code> page, the generated changelog, or the{" "}
    <code>from</code> of a configured redirect.
  </p>

  <h2>Source checks and built-site checks</h2>
  <p>
    Blume has two link checkers, and they read different things.{" "}
    <code>blume validate</code> reads your content files.{" "}
    <code>blume audit</code> reads the HTML in <code>dist/</code> after{" "}
    <code>blume build</code>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col"></th>
        <th scope="col"><code>blume validate</code></th>
        <th scope="col"><code>blume audit</code></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Reads</td>
        <td>Markdown and MDX source</td>
        <td>The built site</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Needs a build</td>
        <td>No</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Broken page link</td>
        <td><code>BLUME_BROKEN_LINK</code>, error</td>
        <td><code>BLUME_AUDIT_LINK_TO_BROKEN</code>, error</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Broken anchor</td>
        <td><code>BLUME_BROKEN_ANCHOR</code>, warning</td>
        <td><code>BLUME_AUDIT_ANCHOR_BROKEN</code>, warning</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Points you to</td>
        <td>File, line, and column</td>
        <td>Page URL and source file</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>validate</code> checks inline links written as{" "}
    <code>[text](target)</code>, even when a formatter wraps the label onto a
    second line, reference-style links (<code>[text][ref]</code>), autolinks
    (<code>&lt;https://…&gt;</code>), image embeds, a component's string{" "}
    <code>href</code>, and lowercase HTML <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> tags. It
    doesn't see <code>{"href={…}"}</code> expressions or a bare URL written
    without link syntax, and it doesn't check your navigation config either;{" "}
    <code>blume doctor</code> does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>audit</code> sees every <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> the build
    rendered, including all of those and the sidebar and header links. It
    reports a broken navigation entry once, not once per page. The cost is a
    build, and findings that name a page rather than a line. Run both:{" "}
    <code>validate</code> first because it's fast and exact, then{" "}
    <code>audit</code> as the backstop.
  </p>

  <h2>Add the pull request check</h2>
  <p>This workflow runs on every pull request and every push to <code>main</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`name: Docs links

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  links:
    name: Links
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v7
        with:
          node-version: 24
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Check links in the source
        run: |
          status=0
          npx blume validate --strict --json > links.json || status=$?
          jq -r '
            .diagnostics[]
            | (if .severity == "info" then "notice" else .severity end) as $level
            | ([ (if .file then "file=\\(.file)" else empty end),
                 (if .line then "line=\\(.line)" else empty end),
                 (if .column then "col=\\(.column)" else empty end),
                 "title=\\(.code)" ] | join(",")) as $props
            | "::\\($level) \\($props)::\\(.message)"
          ' links.json
          exit "$status"
      - run: npx blume build
      - name: Check links in the built site
        run: npx blume audit --only link_to_broken,anchor_broken --fail-on warning`}
    lang="yaml"
    title=".github/workflows/docs-links.yml"
  />
  <p>
    The source step saves the exit code, then uses <code>jq</code> (installed
    on GitHub's Ubuntu runners) to turn each finding in the JSON report into an{" "}
    <code>::error</code> or <code>::warning</code> workflow command with its
    file, line, and column. GitHub shows those as annotations on the lines they
    name. The step then exits with the saved code, so it still fails. On the
    breaking commit, it prints:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`::error file=docs/index.mdx,line=6,col=31,title=BLUME_BROKEN_LINK::Broken link to /installation: no page resolves to /installation.
::warning file=docs/index.mdx,line=6,col=72,title=BLUME_BROKEN_ANCHOR::No anchor target on /configuration matches #api-keys.
::warning file=docs/install.mdx,line=8,col=68,title=BLUME_BROKEN_ANCHOR::No anchor target on /configuration matches #api-keys.`}
    lang="txt"
  />
  <p>
    The paths are relative to the Blume project. If your docs live in a
    subfolder of the repository, set <code>working-directory</code> on the
    steps and add that folder to the front of <code>file=</code>, so the
    annotations land on the right files.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audit step narrows the report to its two link checks.{" "}
    <code>--fail-on warning</code> is what makes the anchor check fail, since
    it's a warning like its <code>validate</code> counterpart. The rest of
    what the audit checks, like titles, descriptions, canonicals, and the
    sitemap, belongs to{" "}
    <a href="/guides/documentation-seo-audit-ci">the SEO audit guide</a>. If
    you add its workflow, its full audit already runs these two checks, so
    drop the build and audit steps here and keep the source check. That way
    each pull request builds the site once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Push the branch and open a pull request. Once the workflow has run, add
    the <code>Links</code> check under "Require status checks to pass before
    merging" in your branch protection rule for <code>main</code>. The workflow
    has no <code>paths</code> filter on purpose: a required check whose
    workflow is skipped stays pending and blocks the merge.
  </p>

  <h2>Probe external links on a schedule</h2>
  <p>
    External links need the network, and their results depend on other
    people's servers. A pull request that touched no links can fail because
    another site is down or unreachable from the runner. So keep them out of
    the required check and probe them on a schedule instead. Blume grades each
    response
    rather than treating every failure as a dead link:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Response</th>
        <th scope="col">Severity</th>
        <th scope="col">What it usually means</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>404 or 410</td>
        <td>Error</td>
        <td>The page is gone. Fix or remove the link.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The host name doesn't resolve</td>
        <td>Error</td>
        <td>The domain is gone, or the link has a typo in it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Any other error status, like 401, 403, 429, or a 5xx</td>
        <td>Warning</td>
        <td>Rate limiting, bot protection, or an outage. Not proof of anything.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The connection is refused or dropped</td>
        <td>Warning</td>
        <td>The site is down, or the runner's network had a problem.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No answer within 10 seconds</td>
        <td>Warning</td>
        <td>A slow or overloaded server.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Blume requests each distinct URL once, eight at a time. It sends a{" "}
    <code>HEAD</code> request and retries with <code>GET</code> when the
    server rejects <code>HEAD</code> (405 or 501) or drops the connection, and
    it follows redirects. This workflow runs every Monday at 06:00 UTC, and on
    demand:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`name: External links

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 6 * * 1"
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  external:
    name: External links
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v7
        with:
          node-version: 24
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Probe external links
        run: |
          status=0
          npx blume validate --external --json > links.json || status=$?
          {
            echo "| Result | Finding | Source |"
            echo "| --- | --- | --- |"
            jq -r '
              .diagnostics[]
              | select(.code == "BLUME_DEAD_LINK")
              | (if .severity == "error" then "Broken" else "Check later" end) as $result
              | "| \\($result) | \\(.message) | \\(.file):\\(.line) |"
            ' links.json
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
          exit "$status"`}
    lang="yaml"
    title=".github/workflows/external-links.yml"
  />
  <p>
    Leave <code>--strict</code> off here. Without it, the job fails only on
    errors, so a 429 adds a "Check later" row to the run's summary and the run
    stays green. With it, every rate limit turns the job red, and people learn
    to ignore it.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a run fails, open each "Broken" URL yourself before you change
    anything. A 404 in your browser is a real dead link, and so is a domain
    that doesn't load anywhere. If one loads for you, rerun the workflow from
    the Actions tab and fix the links that fail twice. Scheduled runs use the latest commit on your
    default branch, and in a public repository GitHub disables them after 60
    days without activity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>blume audit --external</code> probes the links in the built HTML
    with the same grading, as <code>BLUME_AUDIT_EXTERNAL_LINK_BROKEN</code>.
    Use it instead if the scheduled job builds the site anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>Fix and rerun</h2>
  <p>Back on the branch, fix the anchor at its source. Pin the heading's id:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`## Authentication [#api-keys]

Create a key in the dashboard and export it as \`ACME_API_KEY\`.`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/configuration.mdx"
  />
  <p>
    The <code>[#api-keys]</code> marker never renders, and the id stays{" "}
    <code>api-keys</code> whatever the heading says next year. That fixes both
    links at once, plus any on other sites. Pin every heading people are likely
    to link to. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/content/syntax#custom-anchors">Custom anchors</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then point the home page at the new URL, and add a redirect so bookmarks
    and links from outside keep working:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`Start by [installing the CLI](/install), then [create an API key](/configuration#api-keys).`}
    lang="mdx"
    title="docs/index.mdx"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    code={`import { defineConfig } from "blume";

export default defineConfig({
  title: "Acme",
  description: "Send transactional email and SMS with the Acme Messages API.",
  redirects: [{ from: "/installation", to: "/install", status: 308 }],
});`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    Because a redirect's <code>from</code> counts as a valid target, the
    redirect alone would satisfy <code>validate</code>. Update your own links
    anyway: a full <code>blume audit</code> reports internal links that go
    through a redirect as <code>BLUME_AUDIT_LINK_TO_REDIRECT</code>. For moving
    many URLs at once,
    see{" "}
    <a href="/guides/documentation-url-migration-redirects">the URL migration guide</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Rerun the check locally, then push:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`npx blume validate --strict
git commit -am "Fix links after the rename"
git push`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The command prints <code>No broken links found.</code> and exits 0, the
    new push runs the workflow again, and the pull request can merge once the
    check passes.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>A broken anchor doesn't fail the check</h3>
  <p>
    Anchors are warnings in both commands. Add <code>--strict</code> to{" "}
    <code>blume validate</code>, and <code>--fail-on warning</code> to{" "}
    <code>blume audit</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>A link you know is broken isn't reported</h3>
  <p>
    If <code>validate</code> passes, the link is probably one of the forms it
    doesn't read: an <code>{"href={…}"}</code> expression, or a bare URL
    written without link syntax. The audit step after the build catches it.
    Rewriting it as an inline link or a string <code>href</code> moves it back
    into the fast check.
  </p>
  <h3>A link to a custom page is reported as broken</h3>
  <p>
    Blume can't list the paths a dynamic route like{" "}
    <code>pages/compare/[tool].astro</code> serves, so <code>validate</code>{" "}
    reports links to them as broken. Give each path its own static file, as{" "}
    <a href="/docs/advanced/custom-pages">Custom pages</a> describes.
  </p>
  <h3>An example URL fails the external check</h3>
  <p>
    A placeholder domain like <code>api.acme.example</code>, or an internal
    host that only resolves on your network, doesn't resolve on the runner, so{" "}
    <code>--external</code> reports it as an error. A link to{" "}
    <code>localhost</code> is refused, a warning that still fails the check
    under <code>--strict</code>. Skip them with <code>--ignore</code>, which
    takes a glob matched against the link's full URL. Repeat it once per
    pattern, and quote each one so the shell doesn't expand it. In the
    scheduled workflow, the probe line becomes:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`npx blume validate --external --json --ignore "https://api.acme.example/**" --ignore "http://localhost:*/**" > links.json || status=$?`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    <code>*</code> matches within one part of the path and <code>**</code>{" "}
    across parts, so the first pattern covers every link to that host.
    Blume never requests a URL that matches, and it doesn't appear in the
    report. <code>blume audit --external</code> takes the same flag. It only
    applies to external links: internal links are always checked.
  </p>
  <h3>The audit step says there's no build</h3>
  <p>
    <code>No build found at …/dist. Run blume build first.</code> means{" "}
    <code>blume audit</code> ran without a build next to it. Keep it in the
    same job, after <code>npx blume build</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>The audit rejects the check names</h3>
  <p>
    Check ids use underscores: <code>link_to_broken</code>, not{" "}
    <code>link-to-broken</code>. An unknown name fails the run and suggests the
    closest match, so a typo can't empty the report and pass.{" "}
    <code>npx blume audit --list-checks</code> prints every id.
  </p>
  <h3>The strict check fails on something that isn't a link</h3>
  <p>
    <code>validate</code> also reports problems it finds while reading your
    content, like a page that fails to parse, and <code>--strict</code> fails
    on those warnings too. Frontmatter that isn't valid YAML, such as an
    unquoted value holding <code>: </code>, is an error on its own:{" "}
    <code>BLUME_FRONTMATTER_INVALID</code> at the file and line. Fix them
    where the finding points: a page that doesn't parse is a page whose links
    weren't checked.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
